<Layout title={title}>
  <PageTitle title={title} />
  <p class="text-lg mb-10">
    Our system is designed to work with democratic institutions such as parliament
    or city halls in mind. To reflect this, we consider the existence of an
    administrative council (AC), each member of which represents a political party
    present in the parliament or city hall.
    <br \> <br \>
    Whenever a member of this administrative council wishes to perform an action in
    order to add, delete, or read, or otherwise modify a resource that belongs to all
    of the parliament or city hall, they draft the code they wish to execute and then
    submit it to all other administrators on the AC.
    <br \><br \>
    Imagine you're a senior systems administrator. Right now, if someone needs to do something
    critical—like flush firewall rules, modify a production database, or push a security patch—they
    might call you, ask you to run a command with sudo, and you do it. You're a single point of
    failure and a single point of trust. Even on your best day you may do something incorrectly. We
    wished to address this issue while still ensuring that different parliaments or city halls can
    easily integrate the solution into their existing infrastructure.
    <br \><br \>
    To achieve this, we developed a new access control system called <b>Joint User
    Do (Judo)</b>. Judo is an access control mechanism built around two mandatory
    components: the Judo-Daemon and Voters. The Judo-Daemon exposes three API
    commands: Authorize, Execute, and Audit. Both Authorize and Execute accept a
    command as input. Authorize performs a dry run, returning whether the command would
    be approved, while Execute performs the same authorization check and, if approved,
    carries it out. End users interact with the daemon either through a PAM module or
    through an application-level frontend process.
    <br \><br \>
    To determine whether a command should be approved, the Judo-Daemon forwards it to
    the Voters. Each Voter requests manual input from a designated user, who decides
    whether the command should proceed. If a sufficient majority of Voters approve the
    command, it is authorized. The privacy of individual votes is enforced by a Secret
    Sharing-based Secure Multiparty Computation protocol: upon completion, the Judo-Daemon
    learns only whether a majority was reached, and each Voter learns only its own user's
    vote. Critically, these guarantees hold even if some Voter instances behave maliciously
    and deviate from the protocol.
    <br \><br \>
    <b>But what does this mean for you, the administrator?</b>
    <br \><br \>
    Imagine that a junior admin needs to restart a critical service during peak hours. Instead of
    typing <code>sudo systemctl restart payment-processor </code> and entering their password, they
    type <code> judo systemctl restart payment-processor</code>. Nothing happens immediately.
    <br \><br \>
    Instead, you get a popup asking you to vote to approve or reject this command. The popup
    includes the permissions required, the potential impact, and a risk assessment.
    <br \><br \>

    <div class="mx-auto max-w-lg">
            <img src="/JudoVoteRequest.png" alt="Vote Popup" class="mx-auto w-full rounded-lg" />
    </div>

    <br \><br \>

    With this
    information at hand, you cast your vote, as do your colleagues. Every vote is cryprographically
    split into secret pieces, which are distributed to other voters' machines. No single piece can
    reveal how you voted. The pieces can only be reassembled once enough votes are in. This
    guarantees your vote's privacy.
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-lg">
            <img src="/FlowChart.png" alt="Flow Chart" class="w-full rounded-lg" />
    </div>
    Thus, your boss can't pressure you into approving something
    risky and then check how you voted, and an attacker who compromised an admin account cannot
    use that access to run malicious commands in the organization.<br \><br \>

    To maintain accountability, an Audit command enables controlled disclosure of individual
    votes for a previously submitted command. When the daemon receives an Audit request
    referencing a prior Authorize or Execute call, for instance, when a user disputes the
    recorded outcome, the Voters collectively decide whether to disclose all votes cast for
    that command. No information is disclosed unless a majority of voters agree.
    <br \><br \>
    With Judo, we can take one step towards defending against malicious insiders and compromised
    admin accounts while maintining accountability.
  </p>
</Layout>
